  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nUpper level trough over northern California will slowly move east\ninto southern Oregon and southern Idaho through Saturday. This \ntrough will bring a low 10% to 20% chance of light isolated \nshowers across the northern tier of Humboldt and Elko counties. \nThe main impact of this trough though will be to increase the \nwinds across central Nevada for Today, and Thursday. Strong SW \nwinds will be possible across N Nye, Southern Eureka, southern \nLander and White Pine counties as speed of 20 to 35 MPH with gusts\nup to 45 MPH will be possible. A key question for this time frame\nhas been relative humidity, and how far west would a surge of \ntropical moisture moving north into Utah from Arizona would make \nit. Model trends have shifted farther west, and have trended \nhigher with moisture levels as well. Current thinking has this \nquasi dry line setting up along a line from the lee of the Ruby \nmountains south just east of the White Pine/Eureka county line. To\nthe West of this line RH values will be low, near 10% to 15%. to \nthe east RH values climb to near 40% during the afternoons Today \nand Wednesday. As a result of this change Red Flag warnings remain\nin effect for Southern Eureka, Southern Lander, and parts of N \nNye counties though 8PM tonight, and from 11 AM to 8 PM PDT \nThursday. The Fire Weather Watch that was in effect for Thursday \nfor White Pine, central Elko, and eastern Elko counties will be \ncanceled as RH trends are greater that forecast. Although winds \nwill still be strong, the RH will help keep fire weather risks \nelevated, but not critical. Another factor with the higher \nmoisture levels will be chances for isolated showers and \nthunderstorms today and Thursday for eastern Elko and White Pine \ncounties, as a 10% to 30% chance for storms will be possible. \nFriday into the weekend the upper trough will lift into Wyoming, \nthis will push a cool front across Nevada, shifting winds to the \nwest northwest, and allow the pressure gradient to relax and wind \nspeeds to relax back to 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up to 30 MPH \npossible as a shortwave ridge transits NV. Monday a second upper \ntrough will move onshore from the Pacific, this trough will have \nlimited moisture to work with initially, but as it stalls over \nNevada for Tuesday and Wednesday of next week may be able to pull \nin some tropical moisture for a low chance of showers and storms. \nAfter Wednesday model consistency evaporates as the GFS and ECMWF \ndiverge on the upper pattern significantly.",
